    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-4">How to Sign a PDF on Android</h1>

    <p>Unlike iOS, Android does not have a universally standard, built-in PDF markup tool across all manufacturers (Samsung, Google Pixel, etc., all handle PDFs slightly differently).</p>

    <h2>Method 1: Google Drive PDF Viewer</h2>
    <p>If you open a PDF from an email, it will often open in the Google Drive PDF viewer. However, the native editing capabilities are extremely limited and rarely support saving a clean, flattened signature.</p>

    <h2>Method 2: Third-Party Apps (Adobe Acrobat Reader)</h2>
    <p>You can download apps like Adobe Acrobat Reader from the Play Store. They allow you to draw a signature and place it on the document. But, similar to <a href="/compare/adobe-sign">Adobe Sign</a>, they require downloading an app, creating an account, and navigating a cluttered interface.</p>

    <h2>Method 3: The Browser-Based Solution (usignpdf)</h2>
    <p>The fastest and most legally secure way to sign on an Android device is to avoid apps altogether.</p>

    <ol>
        <li>Save the PDF to your Android device (Downloads folder).</li>
        <li>Open Chrome and navigate to <a href="/">usignpdf.com</a>.</li>
        <li>Upload the document directly from your browser.</li>
        <li>Use your touchscreen to draw your signature using our mobile-optimized <a href="/tools/signature-generator">Signature Generator</a>.</li>
        <li>Click complete and download the legally binding, <a href="/security/encryption">encrypted</a> document.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>This method ensures your signature is backed by a full <a href="/security/audit-trails">Audit Trail</a>, making it compliant with global <a href="/legality">legality standards</a>.</p>
